{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18584 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Zizyberenalic acid appears to exert its biological effects through a multi-step mechanism involving the inhibition of DNA polymerase iota. The evidence suggests that Zizyberenalic acid targets the CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which is a key enzyme in the sialylation pathway. This enzyme is subsequently targeted by two structurally distinct compounds, (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and (1S,2R,7R,9S,10S)-6-acetyl-6'-methoxy-1'-methylspiro[4-oxa-12-azatricyclo[7.2.1.02,7]dodec-5-ene-10,3'-indole]-2'-one, both of which are known to inhibit DNA polymerase iota. The shared target of these compounds, DNA polymerase iota, indicates a potential mechanism of action involving the suppression of DNA replication or repair processes. The presence of multiple pathways leading to the same target suggests that Zizyberenalic acid may act through a common mechanism, possibly by modulating the sialylation pathway and thereby affecting DNA polymerase iota activity. The integrated evidence supports a plausible relationship between Zizyberenalic acid and DNA polymerase iota, particularly through the inhibition of the sialyltransferase enzyme, which is a critical component in the regulation of DNA polymerase iota.",
